I'll solve this directly using the residue theorem. No tools needed — this is a standard complex analysis computation.

---

## Evaluation of $\displaystyle\int_0^\infty \frac{\cos x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx$

**Step 1. Choose the complex integrand.**

Set
$$f(z)=\frac{e^{iz}}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}.$$
Since $\cos x = \operatorname{Re}(e^{ix})$ and $\dfrac{1}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}$ is even, we have
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\cos x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx=\operatorname{Re}\!\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{e^{ix}}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx,$$
and the desired integral is half of this (the integrand is even).

**Step 2. Contour and poles.**

Let $C_R$ be the closed contour consisting of $[-R,R]$ on the real axis plus the upper semicircle $\Gamma_R=\{Re^{i\theta}:0\le\theta\le\pi\}$. The integrand has poles where $(z^{2}+1)^{2}=0$, i.e. double poles at $z=\pm i$. Only $z=i$ lies inside $C_R$ (for $R>1$).

**Step 3. The arc integral vanishes.**

On $\Gamma_R$, $\operatorname{Im}z\ge 0$ so $|e^{iz}|=e^{-\operatorname{Im}z}\le 1$, and for $R>1$,
$$\left|\frac{1}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}\right|\le\frac{1}{(R^{2}-1)^{2}}.$$
Hence
$$\left|\int_{\Gamma_R}\frac{e^{iz}}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dz\right|\le\frac{\pi R}{(R^{2}-1)^{2}}\xrightarrow[R\to\infty]{}0.$$

**Step 4. Residue at the double pole $z=i$.**

$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=i}\frac{e^{iz}}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}=\lim_{z\to i}\frac{d}{dz}\!\left[(z-i)^{2}\frac{e^{iz}}{(z-i)^{2}(z+i)^{2}}\right]=\lim_{z\to i}\frac{d}{dz}\!\left[\frac{e^{iz}}{(z+i)^{2}}\right].$$

Compute the derivative:
$$\frac{d}{dz}\!\left[\frac{e^{iz}}{(z+i)^{2}}\right]=\frac{ie^{iz}(z+i)^{2}-2(z+i)e^{iz}}{(z+i)^{4}}=\frac{e^{iz}\bigl[i(z+i)-2\bigr]}{(z+i)^{3}}.$$

At $z=i$: $e^{i\cdot i}=e^{-1}$, $\;z+i=2i$, $\;i(z+i)=i(2i)=-2$, so $i(z+i)-2=-4$, and $(z+i)^{3}=(2i)^{3}=-8i$. Therefore
$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=i}\frac{e^{iz}}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}=\frac{e^{-1}(-4)}{-8i}=\frac{1}{2ie}.$$

**Step 5. Apply the residue theorem.**

$$\oint_{C_R}\frac{e^{iz}}{(z^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dz=2\pi i\cdot\frac{1}{2ie}=\frac{\pi}{e}.$$

Letting $R\to\infty$ and using Step 3,
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{e^{ix}}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{e}.$$

**Step 6. Extract the real part and use evenness.**

Taking real parts,
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\cos x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{e}.$$
The integrand is even, so
$$\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\cos x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{e}=\frac{\pi}{2e}.$$

---

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{\cos x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}}\,dx=\frac{\pi}{2e}}$$

